Appendix I – SGIF History

Rural Fire Information Management System - SGIF

In 2001, a protocol was established between the National Center for Geographic Information (CNIG) and the Directorate-General for Forests (DGF) aimed at producing a computer application designated as the Forest Fire Information Management System (SGIF), with the objective of managing the national database on rural fires.
In the same year, an application was installed in all Prevention and Detection Centers (CPDs), which allowed the computerization of the rural fire data registration process in a systematic and uniform manner and took the first steps towards digital data transmission to a single central database.
The data structure and application development were established in early 2001, with installation and operator training taking place at the beginning of the fire season in June.
From this outset, the system underwent several evolutions as needs arose, driven by technological advancements and institutional reorganizations in the forestry and civil protection sectors.

The following summary presents significant dates in the history of SGIF.

Year 	 Actions
2001	 Protocol established between the National Center for Geographic Information (CNIG) and the Directorate-General for Forests (DGF) for the creation of SGIF. Development of the application and central database. Acquisition of computers and modems. Installation of SGIF application in CPDs.
2002	 Conversion of the central database to SQL Server. Opening to import data directly from the National Fire Service (SNB) application.
2003	 Installation of permanent internet in CPDs. Improvement in the process of importing data to the central database.
2006	 Conversion of the application to a web application. Registration operation is now carried out by the National Republican Guard (GNR), and CPDs are extinguished, and Forest Information Maintenance and Exploitation Teams (EMEIF) are created. Records are now created by the District Command of Relief Operations (CDOS) and imported directly to the central database. Study conducted by COTEC Portugal on surveillance using SGIF data.
2009	 Development of a new version. SGIF starts to have meteorological data associated with fires. SGIF starts to control access to registration and entry into the system. Modules developed for Forestry Technical Offices (GTF). Development of a statistical module for public access.
2011	 Initiation of trend studies.
2012	 Improvement of data consistency verification modules. The location of occurrences is now done by the National Authority for Emergency and Civil Protection (ANEPC). Adjustment of the rekindling concept.
2013	 Update of the new list of parishes. Prototype created for authorization of burns and controlled burns for public access.
2014	 Modules developed for the management of Municipal Forest Defense plans and awareness. A new module is developed for the management of controlled fire activity.
2015	 Current version of SGIF produced. Development of a statistical analysis portal initiated.
2016	 Revision of the data inconsistency evaluation module. Critical site analysis is instituted, with the use of Heat Maps for the distribution of occurrences.
2018	 Areas burned over 10 hectares are digitized by an ICNF team, with the filling of parcel areas done automatically by the perimeter of the area. A new verification is instituted to check if the starting point is inside or outside the burned area perimeter. A new version of the burning and controlled burning registration system is made available to the public. The geographical component of burned area perimeters is stored in an SQL server database.
2021	 The meteorological data series now only has one data source, using the ERA5 global reanalysis series from the European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF).
2022	 The encoding of occurrences becomes the same in the SADO system of ANEPC and SGIF, adopting the SADO encoding. Coding lists for causes and alert lists for occurrences are revised. Various geographic data services with SGIF information are made available.
2024	The meteorological data series was extended until 1980, using the ERA5 global reanalysis series from the European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF).

Important Milestones

At the end of 2001, the process began that led to the conversion of the central database to a SQL Server database, which is still in use today. In 2006, it was necessary to develop a new version due to the changes in responsibilities resulting from the application of concepts established in the National Forest Fire Defense Plan. In 2009, a public access statistical module was launched. In 2018, there was a much greater integration between alphanumeric data and geographic data, and the first public version of the permit to burn registration system was launched.
Where the SGIF Project Fits In
SGIF was part of a broader project, initiated in 1999 at the National Geographic Information Center, called the Emergency Situations Information Network (RISE). The main objectives of the Emergency Situations Information Network were to equip the country with an electronic network of georeferenced information, providing an automatic and up-to-date flow of data related to emergency situations. A subset of this data was made available to the public through the National Geographic Information System website. RISE was a service developed by the Forest Fire Risk Cartography Group (CRIF) of the CNIG, in close cooperation with the National Fire Service, the National Civil Protection Service, the Directorate-General for Forests, the Geographic Institute of the Army, and, at the time of the CNIG's dissolution, agreements were being established with the Directorate-General for Traffic and the National Institute of Medical Emergency. Through a protocol, the adherence of entities that generated records related to emergency situations to the RISE was formalized, ensuring:
•	Remote uploading of information to the database of that institution or to the database of another institution adhering to this protocol.
•	Consultation of cartographic information produced by the Geographic Institute of the Army, made available by RISE applications.
•	The use of information provided by RISE requires institutions to adhere to a set of conditions, including the exclusive use of cartographic data strictly within the scope of emergency or for internal purposes, prohibiting its reproduction or transfer to third parties.
•	This cooperation with other institutions in the network resulted in benefits in terms of sharing experiences, reducing costs, and optimizing benefits.
•	There was an exchange of scientific and technical information related to the management of georeferenced emergency information.
•	There was a strengthening of national intervention capacity in the development of international cooperation projects, which was recognized, for example, by the European Space Agency. In 2001, this agency opened a specific application process to support the development of this network, which resulted in the "PremFIRE" project (CNIG, 2001)

What is the SGIF today and how Is It maintained?

Today, SGIF is a system that collects information on rural fires and generates statistical data on the same topic. It is the national reference database for rural fires. After the creation of the SGIF in 2001, the first major organizational change occurred in 2006. The SGIF became the national reference database for rural fires, which required it to adapt, evolving from an information management application of the DGF to a system that integrates information from the three key institutions of Forest Fire Defense (DFCI) (National Civil Protection Authority - ANPC, National Republican Guard - GNR, and National Forestry Authority (AFN). The second major organizational change took place after 2017, with the introduction of the Integrated Rural Fire Management System. Despite the profound changes during this period, no significant alterations were required in the operational structure of the SGIF. Currently, the SGIF has about 700 users, and the information it produces is used by the Institute for the Conservation of Nature and Forests (ICNF), the National Republican Guard, and the National Emergency and Civil Protection Authority (ANEPC) to produce numerous official reports. Additionally, various scientific studies and these have utilized SGIF data. The responsibility for maintaining the SGIF lies with the ICNF, but since the dissolution of the CNIG in 2002, all developments were carried out by Rui Almeida, João Moreira, Raquel Onofre and Pedro Venâncio. 
SGIF has undergone several improvements over time. In 2010, a thorough analysis of the data was conducted. This process required an exhaustive review of the entire rural fire database, during which some inconsistencies were detected, necessitating the republication of statistical series and base data.
Since 2010, the system has evolved to include stricter internal procedures for data consistency verification. Procedures for associating other information, such as meteorological data (observed and forecasted values), have also been implemented.
The data were re-evaluated again in 2015 and 2022, with the decision to use a new meteorological data series to ensure uniformity in the data source. Whenever it has been possible to associate new data with the core rural fire information, it has enabled the development of new studies, such as the one in this thesis.
One of the current objectives of SGIF is to expand the set of users accessing its information. To this end, new modules for unregistered users are being developed. Examples include partnerships being developed with Pordata and the National Institute of Statistics, where new approaches to disseminating information are being created.
